For Developers
**************

.[perex]
How to configure Texy, change its output, teach it a new syntax or build your own tool on top of it. If you're new to Texy, go through the [introduction |@home] first; this page picks up where it leaves off.


[Configuration | configuration]
-------------------------------

All the settings in one place: which syntaxes are enabled, what the output HTML looks like, typography, headings, and safe mode for visitor comments.


[Texy in Latte | latte]
-----------------------

How to convert Markdown or Texy syntax right in Latte templates, with the `|texy` filter or the `{texy}` tag.


[Customizing Elements | custom-handlers]
----------------------------------------

How to change what an existing syntax produces: a YouTube video instead of an image, a class on every heading, a change to every link in the document.


[Adding Custom Syntax | custom-syntax]
--------------------------------------

How to teach Texy a completely new syntax, such as `@@jane` mentions or warning boxes.


[Source Text Tools | source-tools]
----------------------------------

How to write a program that checks texts written in Texy or edits them in bulk, leaving the rest of the text untouched.


[Architecture and Principles | architecture]
--------------------------------------------

What happens to a text from the moment Texy receives it until the HTML is ready: the processing steps, the document tree, and the places where you can hook in.


[Upgrading from Texy 3 | upgrading]
-----------------------------------

What changed in version 4, which parts of your configuration keep working, and how to rewrite handlers and custom syntaxes.

{{maintitle: Texy for Developers: Using the PHP Library}}
{{description: Developer documentation for Texy: from basic use of the PHP library all the way to advanced techniques for extending it and adding your own syntax.}}
